<Base title="Predicting Defensive Opportunities | Bence Supola"><div class="container"><div class="case-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Case study 01 · Spatial analytics / Machine learning</div><h1>Predicting Opportunities Before They Happen</h1><p class="lead">Recognizing defensive gaps in basketball through geometric feature engineering and interpretable machine learning.</p><div class="skills"><span class="skill">Tracking data</span><span class="skill">Gradient boosting</span><span class="skill">SHAP</span><span class="skill">Feature engineering</span></div></div><div class="stats"><div class="stat"><strong>81.8%</strong><span>Reported balanced accuracy</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>139 ms</strong><span>Mean detection lead time for evaluated cuts</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>25 Hz</strong><span>Tracking-data sampling frequency</span></div></div><div class="prose"><h2>The challenge</h2><p>Defensive rotations can open a path to the basket for only a fraction of a second. The objective was to detect these emerging opportunities from player-tracking data, including situations in which an offensive player did not actually exploit the opening.</p><h2>The analytical idea</h2><p>Rather than using raw coordinates alone, we represented the geometry between an offensive player, their defender and the basket with purpose-built features. Changes in these spatial relationships allowed the model to capture the formation of a potential cutting lane.</p></div><figure class="figure"><img src="/images/defensive-sequence.png" alt="Three-panel reconstruction of a drive, dribble pass and shot from the defensive-gaps analysis." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Unpublished visualization from the defensive-gaps analysis, originally created for LinkedIn. Three consecutive phases illustrate tracking-based reconstruction of an offensive sequence; this is not presented as a model prediction.</figcaption></figure><div class="prose"><h2>From features to predictions</h2><p>A gradient-boosting classifier was trained to distinguish open from denied cutting opportunities. Model interpretation used feature relationships and explanation techniques including SHAP. Evaluation considered not only classification performance but also the timing of detections relative to actual cuts.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Core insight</strong><p style="margin:6px 0 0">The goal was not simply to recognize what a player did. It was to identify an opportunity the player could have acted on.</p></div></div><div class="prose"><h2>Understanding feature interactions</h2><p>Feature importance tells us which variables matter to the model overall. But the emergence of a cutting opportunity depends on how geometric relationships change <em>together</em>. Two-feature partial dependence plots reveal these interactions by showing how the fitted model responds to combinations of feature values, averaged over the other inputs.</p><p>For example, a defender’s distance from the cutting path (<code>h</code>) must be interpreted together with their depth relative to the basket (<code>c</code>) and changes in their position. The four surfaces below illustrate why individual feature rankings alone cannot fully describe the learned decision boundary.</p></div><figure class="figure figure-wide"><a href="/images/defensive-partial-dependence.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open full-resolution partial dependence plots"><img src="/images/defensive-partial-dependence.png" alt="Four two-feature partial dependence surfaces: c_change versus c, h_change versus c_change, h versus c, and h versus h_change." loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><strong>Two-feature partial dependence plots.</strong> Interactions between engineered spatial features in the defensive-gap detection model. Color scales differ across panels; the plotted model responses should not be interpreted as causal effects. Reproduced from Figure 4 in Supola, Hoch &amp; Baca (2023), <em>PLOS ONE</em>, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0281467">doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0281467</a>, licensed under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. Click to view at full resolution.</figcaption></figure><div class="prose"><h2>Results and relevance</h2><p>The published study reported 81.8% balanced accuracy and detection, on average, about 139 ms ahead of observed cut initiation in the evaluated cases. These figures describe the study's evaluation setting, not a general guarantee for new games or leagues.</p><p>The transferable contribution is a workflow for converting domain-specific spatial relationships into interpretable features and testing whether those features reveal meaningful events in dynamic systems.</p><h2>My contribution</h2><p>I implemented the research programming and technical workflow. The scientific study, interpretation and publication were collaborative efforts with my coauthors.</p><p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0281467">Read the peer-reviewed paper ↗</a></p><p><a href="/#work">← Back to selected work</a></p></div></div></Base>
